  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 PM MDT Tue Sep 15 2026\n\nLots of moisture moved into the region on Tuesday and that \nmoisture trend will continue on Wednesday through Friday. Each day\nwill see scattered afternoon and evening thunderstorms, as \nmoisture rotates up from norther Mexico on the west side of the \nupper level ridge centered over the southeast U.S. At the same \ntime, an upper level trough will slowly be approaching the region \nfrom the west. As I mentioned the circulations around those two \nfeatures, will continue the conveyor belt of moisture from the \nsouth. All we need is some trigger to fire off the storms. Sadly \nno stronger trigger is evident so we will have to do with the \ninstability due to daytime heating and the weak short waves that \nwill also rotate up from the south. While we don't have a strong \ntrigger for thunderstorms, I do think we will see scattered \nthunderstorms and with the above average precipitable water values\n(PW's), any storms that do form will be capable of producing some\nheavy rain leading to flash flooding. A flash flood watch is in \neffect for the mountains and locations west of the Rio Grande on \nWednesday. The moisture and rain chances will continue on into \nFriday and Saturday, with a slow eastward drift of the main access\nof moisture as the trough to our west slowly pushes in our \ndirection. Sunday looks dry as we will be on the backside of one \ntrough, but by Monday, we will see the return of moisture from the\nsouth as another upper level trough approaches from the west so \nmore rain will be in the forecast for the start of next week.\n\nLooking at temperature, as you might expect with all the clouds \nand moisture, our hot temperature for the last several months are \nlong gone and we will see much more seasonal high temperature for \nthe middle and end of the week. High temperatures for Wednesday \nthrough Friday will run a little below average out west and a \nlittle above average east of the Rio Grande. Those seasonal \ntemperatures look to continue into next weekend. Then with the \nreturn of moisture and rain chances for next week, we may even see\nour high temperatures running a few degrees below average, which \nis something I haven't talked about in quite a long time.",
